    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper uses vector ablation as an over-refusal mitigation method and reports its performance, but does not establish that the ablated vector is the harmful-minus-harmless difference-in-means refusal direction in the claim.",
    "gate_note": "The paper describes Vector Ablation (VA) as relying on a false-refusal vector and reports its attack, over-refusal, and capability metrics. These results concern that mitigation method; the paper does not identify its vector with the specific direction in the claim. Other defense-method results do not apply, add, or measure that direction.",
    "test_reason": "X-Boundary proposes a representation-level jailbreak defense. Vector Ablation (VA; Wang et al. 2024, a false-refusal vector ablation) appears only as a baseline over-refusal mitigation method in Appendix C.1, Tables 3–4. The paper gives no extraction details and does not identify VA's vector with the harmful-minus-harmless difference-in-means refusal direction. It reports no ablation or addition of that direction and no rate of refusal caused by it. Its reasoning-model results (Fig. 8) evaluate X-Boundary and other defenses, not the direction. No version is tested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Partly right but incomplete. The VA statements match Appendix C.1: VA helps on the vanilla model, is less effective after defense fine-tuning, weakens defense, and is ineffective for CB-like methods. The paper's main conclusion is different: existing defenses lack a precise boundary between harmful and boundary-safe representations. X-Boundary's explicit separate loss is reported to cut ASR while keeping over-refusal low and capability intact, including on R1-distilled reasoning models.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper applies a cited vector-ablation over-refusal mitigation method; it does not report using the original harmful-minus-harmless difference-in-means procedure or the claim's all-layer, all-position ablation.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported VA results vary by model setting: vanilla, SFT + VA, and CB + VA.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "VA, a cited false-refusal vector ablation, behaves differently by model setting on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct. On the vanilla model it lowers over-refusal (OKTest 19.33→11.00) with little change in ASR. After SFT it lowers over-refusal but raises ActorAttack ASR 21.00→43.50. After CB it changes over-refusal little.",
        "proposed_claim": "Single-vector false-refusal ablation reduces over-refusal in vanilla models but weakens robustness after safety fine-tuning. It is ineffective for defenses without a fixed refusal template.",
